---
title: Your first C++ project
description: Create a C++ application, add a named module, and build, run, and test it in Neovim.
section: Tutorials
order: 1
updated: '2026-10-14'
---

Create a C++ application named `engine`, add a named module, and use a compiler error to learn the quickfix workflow. By the end, the application will print `Answer: 42` and pass its CTest test.

## Load Rivet and check the tools

Use Neovim 0.11 or newer and [install Rivet through your plugin manager](/docs/start-here/#install-in-neovim). Restart after changing the configuration. Run `:RivetDoctor` to check the CMake/compiler tools used by this project. This walkthrough needs CMake 3.30+, Ninja 1.11+, and a C++26 compiler with named-module dependency scanning. On macOS, select Homebrew LLVM `clang++` and its matching `clang-scan-deps`; `clang-format` is optional. See [tested toolchains](/docs/compatibility/) for details.

## Create the project

In a parent directory of your choice, create a folder and open Neovim inside it:

```sh
mkdir engine
cd engine
nvim
```

Run `:pwd` to confirm the destination, then `:RivetNew` and use these choices. If a saved-profile menu appears, select **New choices** first:

| Prompt               | Choice for this walkthrough                   |
| -------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Language             | `cpp` (C++)                                   |
| Create project in    | Current folder                                |
| Project name         | `engine` (inferred from the folder, editable) |
| Git repository       | Skip Git initialization                       |
| Project license      | No license yet                                |
| File comment headers | No header                                     |
| C++ standard         | C++26                                         |
| Workload profile     | General C++                                   |
| Project kind         | Application + reusable core                   |
| C++ interface        | Named modules                                 |
| Module files         | Interface + implementation                    |
| Standard library     | Standard-library headers                      |
| Project layout       | Folder by feature                             |
| Formatting style     | LLVM                                          |
| Header protection    | `#pragma once`                                |
| C++ compiler         | Your module-capable `clang++` executable      |

Ninja is selected automatically for named modules. This example uses `#include` for the standard library.

Inspect the final file tree, Git action, and previews. Press `c` or Ctrl-S to create the project, or `q` to cancel. Everything is created directly in `engine`; no nested `engine/engine` folder is added. Enter selects options in ordinary pickers; the final review deliberately uses a separate apply key.

## Build, run, and test the starter

Open the generated application:

```vim
:edit app/main.cpp
:RivetTask build
```

Choose **debug** on the first task. Output appears as configure and build run. A failure stops the workflow; the output explains which step failed. A successful final step ends with `Exit status: 0`.

Run `:RivetTask run`. It configures and builds before launching the application, which prints:

```text
Worker 0: [0, 4)
```

Then run `:RivetTask test`. CTest runs `engine_test`; the starter should report one passing test. It checks the range-partitioning example and does not require an external test framework.

Use `q` to close task output. Rivet remembers the preset; `:RivetPreset` changes it. Wait for each task to finish before starting the next.

## Add a single-file module

Run `:RivetFile`, select **C++** in the language picker, choose **Module interface**, select the `engine_core` target, use the name `number`, and name the module `engine.number`. The review should add `src/core/number.cppm` and register it in the core target's `CXX_MODULES` file set. Approve with `c`.

Open `src/core/number.cppm` and replace its contents with:

```cpp
export module engine.number;

export namespace engine {
constexpr int answer() {
  return 42;
}
}
```

The module contains its definition, so it needs no separate implementation file.

Replace `app/main.cpp` with:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

import engine.core;
import engine.number;

int main() {
  std::cout << "Answer: " << engine::answer() << '\n';
  const auto range = engine::partition_work(10, 3, 0);
  std::cout << "Worker 0: [" << range.begin << ", " << range.end << ")\n";
  return 0;
}
```

Save both files with `:wall`, then run `:RivetTask run`. You should see:

```text
Answer: 42
Worker 0: [0, 4)
```

In `tests/core_test.cpp`, add `import engine.number;` alongside the existing import. At the start of `main`, add `if (engine::answer() != 42) { return 1; }`.

Save and run `:RivetTask test`. The test should pass with the new check included. Rivet builds saved files, so save before starting each task.

## Follow a compiler error

In `app/main.cpp`, temporarily change `engine::answer()` to `engine::answr()`, save, and run `:RivetTask build`. The build should fail and report the missing member.

Run `:copen`. Rivet collects GCC/Clang-style file diagnostics while output streams. Select an entry and press Enter to jump to its file and location; `:cnext` and `:cprevious` move between entries. Relative compiler paths resolve from the build directory.

Correct the typo, save, and run `:RivetTask repeat`. The build should succeed. Run `:RivetTask test` once more to check the project tests. A fresh workflow gets a fresh quickfix list, so old errors do not remain in the new result.

## Next steps

You have created, extended, and tested a C++ module. Use [Files and targets](/docs/templates/) for another component, [Modules and headers](/docs/modules/) for interface choices, or [Doxygen](/docs/doxygen/) to document the API.
